1
00:00:00,507 --> 00:00:01,443
Here's something weird:

2
00:00:01,769 --> 00:00:04,276
adult cats almost never meow at each

3
00:00:04,126 --> 00:00:04,610
other.

4
00:00:04,852 --> 00:00:06,610
The meow is a sound they save

5
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:07,693
for one species.

6
00:00:07,936 --> 00:00:08,490
Us.

7
00:00:09,128 --> 00:00:11,112
Kittens meow to call their mother when

8
00:00:10,962 --> 00:00:12,600
they are cold or hungry.

9
00:00:12,843 --> 00:00:14,671
But as they grow up, most cats

10
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,814
stop meowing to other cats completely.

11
00:00:17,486 --> 00:00:20,303
Between themselves, cats talk with scent, body

12
00:00:20,165 --> 00:00:21,993
language, hisses and growls.

13
00:00:22,212 --> 00:00:24,243
A tail held straight up says hello.

14
00:00:24,462 --> 00:00:26,505
A slow blink is a friendly signal.

15
00:00:27,128 --> 00:00:28,469
So why do they meow at us?

16
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:30,957
Because we're terrible at reading cat language.

17
00:00:31,188 --> 00:00:32,814
We miss the tail, we miss the

18
00:00:32,676 --> 00:00:33,421
ears.

19
00:00:33,652 --> 00:00:35,100
But we always notice noise.

20
00:00:35,716 --> 00:00:37,616
So cats kept the kitten sound and

21
00:00:37,478 --> 00:00:38,581
turned it into a tool.

22
00:00:38,835 --> 00:00:40,842
Basically, your cat treats you like a

23
00:00:40,776 --> 00:00:43,521
big, slightly clueless mother cat.

24
00:00:44,128 --> 00:00:45,159
And it gets smarter.

25
00:00:45,378 --> 00:00:47,897
In two thousand nine, researchers discovered the

26
00:00:47,747 --> 00:00:48,981
solicitation purr:

27
00:00:49,271 --> 00:00:51,421
a purr with a hidden cry inside,

28
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:52,981
close to the pitch of a human

29
00:00:52,831 --> 00:00:53,695
baby's cry.

30
00:00:54,328 --> 00:00:55,895
Cats use it mostly when they want

31
00:00:55,745 --> 00:00:56,335
food.

32
00:00:56,566 --> 00:00:58,228
And it works, because our brains find

33
00:00:58,078 --> 00:01:00,097
that sound really hard to ignore.

34
00:01:00,716 --> 00:01:01,402
Even better:

35
00:01:01,764 --> 00:01:04,009
each cat builds its own vocabulary with

36
00:01:03,859 --> 00:01:04,485
its human.

37
00:01:04,728 --> 00:01:06,759
Owners tend to understand their own cat's

38
00:01:06,609 --> 00:01:09,343
meows, but struggle with a stranger's cat.

39
00:01:10,046 --> 00:01:12,541
Feral cats that live without people meow

40
00:01:12,391 --> 00:01:13,374
much less.

41
00:01:13,581 --> 00:01:15,612
A meow only makes sense when there

42
00:01:15,462 --> 00:01:16,743
is a human around to hear it.

43
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:18,869
So the next time your cat meows

44
00:01:18,719 --> 00:01:20,036
at you at five in the morning,

45
00:01:20,148 --> 00:01:20,750
remember:

46
00:01:21,088 --> 00:01:22,715
that sound was made just for you.

47
00:01:23,316 --> 00:01:24,514
It's not just noise.

48
00:01:24,757 --> 00:01:26,990
It's your cat speaking the only language

49
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:28,062
it thinks you understand.

50
00:01:28,695 --> 00:01:30,107
What does your cat's 'I want food'

51
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:30,964
meow sound like?

52
00:01:31,183 --> 00:01:32,298
Write it in the comments.
